hallenges so first of all something  that you've all probably heard about is  containers so a container is a logical  concept that uses a collection of  features introduced into the Linux  kernel to isolate processes from one  another and to make sure that they don't  use more CPU or memory than is allocated  to them so basically it's like  lightweight virtualization that uses the  host machines operating system although  it's been very popular in the past 10  years container technologies actually by  no means new it's been around since the  1980s in its earliest versions but it  didn't really take off until  2006 when Google contributed a Linux  kernel patch that introduced control  groups which are one of the key features  that enable modern container technology  containers didn't get particularly  popular until a company called docker  made it very easy for people to create  container images that could be shared  through repositories to to store them  and to run them remotely and yes you can  probably tell the container space has  certainly exploded since then so  containers solved the first challenge  that we have they isolate the process  and they also make it very easy to  deploy applications that have  dependencies such as Python data science  applications you might have dependency  on numpy scifi lots of other different  things so containers solve those two  things for us so for Jupiter hub  installation we would need a container  to run a jupiter notebook server for  each user depending on the number of  users on your platform this could be in  the thousands we also need something to  run the hub and the proxy and to make  sure that those are running through the  whole duration when our system should be  up so for a small deployment of five or  six people you might be able to do this  manually packaging them up in containers  then logging in to whatever server is  going to be serving this and and running  the necessary commands there but for  larger scale deployments you need to  think about what if your machine fails  whether it's an on-premise data center  or a public cloud it's possible that it  would fail and you would have to default  to another availability zone what if you  have to redeploy something while you  have active users what if you have to  load balanced users or redirect them to  another data center so those are the  kinds of things that it would be very  hard for you to do if you had lots of  users when you had to do this manually  so in the infrastructure software  engineering space there have been  several solutions to this problem of  managing large-scale containerized  applications that need to have high  availability and these are known as  container orchestrators they take care  of two main things for you so first they  manage a cluster which is a collection  of cloud-based or on-premise machines  the term the applications point of view  look like a single machine so your  jupiter hub single notebook server  shouldn't actually care  which actual physical or virtual  machine' they're being deployed to and  what handles this deployment is the  orchestrator it's schedules and manages  containers it makes sure that there is  adequate compute power on the virtual  machine where the container is scheduled  and it can also do things like failover  and act as this help healing system so  if a node in your cluster becomes  unhealthy for whatever reason the  orchestrator can deal with this so if  you go back to the Jupiter hub project  there are many many container  orchestrators that are in use these days  but the Jupiter hub project has chosen  to go with kubernetes which is an open  source orchestration system that was  first developed at Google to handle  their large-scale or container  application loads so what kubernetes  does in a nutshell is it has a master  component this component manages a  cluster of nodes which are for example  virtual machines on your favorite cloud  provider and it takes care of scheduling  containers scheduling applications -  load balancing failover and making sure  that the state of the cluster is always  in the desired form so there are two key  things that have been very interesting  about kubernetes so first of all  kubernetes has introduced what's known  as declarative infrastructure  definitions so ten years or so ago when  you were first preparing your server to  deploy your application you could  potentially SSH into the machine and  then run a batch script or run a series  of commands that would prepare your  machine to host your application so  these days that's known as an imperative  infrastructure definition and it's no  longer in use so instead of it's no  longer an used widely I should say I'm  sure there are still places that that  provision infrastructure in these ways  but what's different about kubernetes is  that instead of defining the steps that  should be taken you define what you want  the state of the infrastructure to be so  for example I could say I want 3  replicas of Jupiter hub running but I  don't tell kubernetes how to get to 3  States instead kubernetes continuously  monitors my Jupiter hub deployment and  when it detects that for example one of  the jupiter hub processes has failed  it's going  try to be self-healing and bring up  another process to replace it the second  thing that's important is obstructing of  infrastructure so instead of dealing  with the actual physical virtual  machines and network interfaces we're  dealing with abstract objects such as  pods which represent the service a  process or services which represent  logical sets of pods and an access  policy that users can use to access them  so in theory at least this should be  make it easier for people who aren't  very familiar with infrastructure to run  applications on kubernetes so this is  basically how you define a service in  kubernetes if you write a configuration  file and then you deploy it to the  server for small applications once again  this is okay but if you have to write a  lot of llamó you will very soon have a  bad headache  so what kubernetes does is it has a  package manager called helm and there  are two components in it one of these  components sits on your client machine  and the other one tiller is on the  cluster so your client machine receipt  your client machine talks to tiller and  tells it how to deploy applications on  your kubernetes cluster and this is also  the route that the open source Jupiter  how project has chosen to go to so they  basically provide a home chart for you  which makes it really easy for you to  deploy your Jupiter have applications  once you have your cluster up and  running so to prepare for this  presentation I try to deploy Jupiter hub  on three different commercial cloud  providers and compare a little bit the  costs and ease of deployment in doing  this so as you might know Google they  sort of have a monopoly are on  kubernetes services at least until  recently so they have an offering called  google kubernetes engine which is a  managed solution this means you don't  actually need to know too much about  running kubernetes because Google takes  care of that for you so the good thing  about this that things usually work out  of the box you can check out the Jupiter  hub helm chart from github run a few  commands on your local shell and you'll  have a Jupiter hub a working Jupiter hub  running quite quickly the other nice  thing about this also is that you only  pay for the Google compute engine  instances that make up your node your  nodes you don't actually have to pay for  the machines that run the kubernetes  master itself so if you want to try out  a small cluster for your Jupiter hub  deployment with just three nodes and  each of them is one of our Cho CPU in a  bit of RAM you can get away with about  an 80 euro bill per month and this will  be good for a small demo maybe like five  to ten notebooks and nothing to CPU  intensive so next one is AWS so until  recently AWS had no easy way of running  a kubernetes cluster instead you had two  two options if you are feeling a bit  adventurous and wanted to do some  do-it-yourself deployment of kubernetes  so you can use a guide produced by a  company called hefty oh and a  CloudFormation template which is  Amazon's way of writing configuration  for for infrastructure components or you  could use a command line tool called  cops which makes it quite easy to to  deploy a kubernetes cluster that you  manage yourself on AWS you know the two  downsides of this are is that you will  have to sort of be an expert in how to  deal with kubernetes problems in case  something goes down and you have to  debug it whereas with Google Cloud you  don't really deal with that stuff  in addition you will also have to pay  for the virtual machine that runs the  kubernetes master and it's quite a large  machine so that will definitely add to  your bill last but not least is Azure  container service which is quite similar  to the Google cloud offering in the  sense that it's also a managed service  so you don't have to actually deal with  installing kubernetes or deploying any  of the components it is a bit new on the  market so there are still a few hiccups  but overall it works quite well so I  also try to deploy Jupiter hub onto here  and it works pretty much out of the box  the very nice thing about helm here is  that once you have set up your  kubernetes cluster your Jupiter hub  application is pretty much portable  across cloud providers the only thing  that's different is the actual process  of setting up the cluster once you have  that installed you can just use helm and  all of a sudden you can deploy to pretty  much any cloud provider that it supports  kubernetes so I try to compare the is  your container service with the  - vignetting sanguine in terms of cost  and although the two providers don't  have machines that are exact replicas of  each other I found this one which was  quite close to the one I used for the  Google Cloud cluster and it's also three  nodes with one core and a bit of RAM and  the bill for a month if you leave it  running 24/7 is 98 heroes so they are  quite close in terms of cost so if you  work in a company and you want to try  out Jupiter hub on Google Cloud  I suggest coming up with this kind of  decision matrix that will help you make  a technical decision so when when I work  with teams that need to make a technical  decision we usually write the things  that we care about is it easy to deploy  how well can I use home charts what is  it going to cost me and so forth and  then we go across the cloud providers  and we see how well they check our  expectations against it so for example  at the moment I probably would not go  with AWS until they have a managed  kubernetes service just because you need  another team to take care of your  kubernetes deployment or at least  someone who knows what they're doing so  when I gave this talk at PI data London  a few weeks ago a lots of people came up  to me to talk about scaling and whether  how easy it would be to run this at a  very very large scale so the things that  you need to think about here first are  how many users you will have and what  kinds of workloads will they be running  if users are just using this to learn  Python for example it's very different  than doing some large-scale matrix  computation or computing fractals you  also need to think about how the spike  of load is distributed so if you're  running a class it's very likely that  everyone will want to log in at once if  you are deploying this for a team it  could be true that the load is more  distributed across the day but either  way the open source Jupiter hub project  has provided a handy Python notebook  that you can use to estimate your costs  and it's linked on the slide there's  also a very nice case study from a data  science class called data eight which  had a thousand plus users and the  notebook that's been  the link will tell you a bit about how  they've budgeted for their class to make  sure that they stay within limits last  but not least on this slide is a very  very interesting talk about by Scott  Sanderson who works at canto pian I  don't know how many of you have heard  about canto pian but it's a community  for doing algorithmic trading in python  and they run their data science  infrastructure on jupiter hub and so in  this talk he talked about running  jupiter hub for a hundred thousand users  it's quite impressive in addition to  mentioning how they do scaling with  multiple jupiter hub instances and load  balancing users between different  jupiter hubs scott also talks about  managing state with Postgres so  definitely recommend watching this talk  if you're planning to to scale up  jupiter hub to hundreds of thousands of  users so in summary we've seen what the  Jupiter project is what the ecosystem is  and how to make it easy for you to  provision large-scale Jupiter have  deployments with Jupiter hub we've taken  a look at some of the things that go on  in Jupiter hub under the hood and also  the infrastructure components such as  containers kubernetes and others that  make it very easy for us to deploy  Jupiter hub in the cloud so I believe  I'm doing ok for time and there is time  for questions so this is all I had and  I'll be happy to take your questions now  or through email you're perfect in time  thank you  I said they decided my side but myself I  found it very useful so thank you again  and it does anybody in the room have a  question for her sorry yeah thanks for a  nice talk I'm gonna ask us something  maybe a little bit off-topic but it's it  is there any chance of running Jupiter  hub on Windows these days as I work in a  hospital and they believe in Windows so  do they believe in Windows 10 not yet  there is windows subsystem for Linux  which is what I actually have running so  I think this might 